# Architecture

## Purpose

Give every contributor (human or AI) a precise mental model of how LensJS is layered so
changes land in the right package, reuse the right primitive, and never break a boundary.

## Why it exists

LensJS is a published, framework-agnostic observability library (Laravel Telescope for Node).
It captures requests, DB queries, cache ops, sent mail, and exceptions and shows them in a
bundled dashboard. Its value depends on stable boundaries: `@lensjs/core` is the engine,
adapters are thin framework glue, handlers are thin driver glue, and none of them may leak
into each other. Break a boundary and every downstream package breaks.

## The layers (dependency flow)

```
@lensjs/date  <—  @lensjs/core  <—  @lensjs/{express,fastify,nestjs,adonis}
                       ^                        (adapters)
                       |
                @lensjs/watchers  (handlers: prisma/kysely/sequelize/nodemailer)
```

- `@lensjs/core` — the engine. Abstract contracts (`Watcher`, `Store`, `Adapter`), the `Lens`
  orchestrator, request context, DI container, the typed emitter, the API controller +
  route table, the default queued SQLite store, and the bundled React dashboard.
- `@lensjs/<framework>` — adapters. Translate one framework's request/response/error/DB
  primitives into core calls. They NEVER re-implement capture, storage, or routing logic.
- `@lensjs/watchers` (folder `packages/handlers`) — driver integrations. Translate one
  ORM/mailer (Prisma, Kysely, Sequelize, Nodemailer) into core's neutral data shapes.
- `@lensjs/date` — the only place time/formatting logic lives (UTC producers + UI presenters).
- `@lensjs/typescript-config` — shared tsconfig presets.

Dependencies only ever point LEFT (toward core/date). Core must not import an adapter or a
handler; an adapter must not import another adapter's internals; a handler must not import a
framework.

## Core orchestration (how it boots)

`Lens.start()` runs a fixed sequence — see [lens.ts](mdc:packages/core/src/core/lens.ts):

1. bail early if `!config.enabled`;
2. `bindContainerDeps()` registers the store + `uiConfig` singletons in the DI
   [container.ts](mdc:packages/core/src/context/container.ts);
3. `adapter.setWatchers(...).setup()` wires framework instrumentation;
4. `adapter.registerRoutes(getRoutes(...).apiRoutes)` — **routes are defined in core**, the
   adapter only mounts them;
5. `adapter.serveUI(getUiPath(), config.path, getUiConfig())` serves the dashboard.

Access the store only via the container: `getStore()` from
[context.ts](mdc:packages/core/src/context/context.ts). Correlate events to a request via the
`AsyncLocalStorage` `lensContext` in [async_context.ts](mdc:packages/core/src/utils/async_context.ts).

## Neutral data contracts live in core

`RequestEntry`, `QueryEntry`, `CacheEntry`, `MailEntry`, `ExceptionEntry`, `WatcherTypeEnum`,
`LensConfig`, `Paginator<T>`, `ApiResponse<T>` are all defined in
[types/index.ts](mdc:packages/core/src/types/index.ts) (mail in
[types/mail.ts](mdc:packages/core/src/types/mail.ts)). Adapters and handlers only PRODUCE these
shapes; they never redefine them. Provider unions are derived from core via `Extract<...>`.

## Public vs internal (the contract surface)

- PUBLIC = the package `exports` map + what the package barrel re-exports. For core that is
  [src/index.ts](mdc:packages/core/src/index.ts) (`Lens`, `LensAdapter`, `LensStore`,
  `LensWatcher`, the watchers, the stores, `lensUtils`, `lensExceptionUtils`, `lensEmitter`,
  `lensContext`, `getLensStore`, types). Treat every public symbol and every `exports` entry
  as a contract.
- INTERNAL = everything not re-exported from the barrel (e.g. `Container`, `ApiController`,
  raw files under `context/`, `mixins/`). Internals may change freely; public symbols may not
  without a major changeset.

## Extension points (the only sanctioned ways to extend)

- New signal to capture  -> add a `Watcher` (see `watchers.mdc`).
- New persistence backend -> extend `Store` and pass it to `Lens.setStore()` (see `stores.mdc`).
- New web framework       -> extend `LensAdapter` (see `adapters.mdc`).
- New ORM/mailer          -> add a handler in `@lensjs/watchers` (see `packages.mdc`).

## What must NEVER be coupled

- Adapters <-> `Store`: adapters never touch a store directly; they call watchers, which call
  `getStore()`.
- Handlers <-> a web framework: a handler for Prisma must work under any adapter.
- Core <-> a specific framework/ORM: core stays framework- and driver-agnostic.
- UI <-> a hardcoded mount path: the dashboard discovers its base + API paths at runtime.

## Golden rules for every change

1. Search for an existing implementation/utility first (`lensUtils`, `@lensjs/date`, existing
   watchers/adapters/handlers). Reuse it. Never duplicate logic.
2. Follow existing repository naming and file placement (see `typescript.mdc`, `packages.mdc`).
3. Preserve the architecture and boundaries above. Reject changes that blur them.
4. Keep public APIs backward compatible; a breaking change needs a `major` changeset.
5. Add/update tests for any behavior change (see `testing.mdc`).
6. Update docs when public behavior changes (see `documentation.mdc`).
7. Keep changes minimal and scoped; do not opportunistically refactor unrelated code.
8. Add a changeset for any user-facing `@lensjs/*` change (see `release.mdc`).
9. Never log or persist secrets/PII (see `security.mdc`).
10. Explain the architectural reasoning behind non-trivial changes.

## AI must never

- Put framework- or ORM-specific code in `@lensjs/core`.
- Re-implement core capture/store/context/routing logic inside an adapter or handler.
- Import across packages with relative paths (e.g. `../../core/dist/...`) — always use
  `@lensjs/*`. (An existing offender in
  [define_config.ts](mdc:packages/adonis/src/define_config.ts) is tech debt, not a pattern.)
- Widen or break a public type/symbol to make an internal change easier.
